{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>If its owner is with it, etc.</b> There is for us to examine in this law: is one judged by this law in the World to Come, when the owner of the deposit comes — that is, the Master of the universe, who deposited the soul in the hand of man to guard, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:9) ״וּשְׁמֹר נַפְשְׁךָ מְאֹד״ — when He comes to claim His deposit from each and every person, and it is found stolen, or broken, or captured, or dead? For all these conditions are found among transgressors of transgression. There is one whose soul is stolen from him through an impure encounter, and there is one who is deliberate in a transgression, and a limb of the limbs of the soul designated corresponding to the commandment he violated is damaged, and sometimes by committing a sin from among those punishable by excision or death at the hands of the court, he causes death to the soul, as it is said (Genesis 17:14) ״וְנִכְרְתָה הַנֶּפֶשׁ״, and sometimes he causes it captivity, in the manner of their saying (Zohar 2:95a) the oppressed souls, etc. And when the owner of the deposit stands and presses a claim that is heard: ״Give Me the soul as I gave it to you,״ as it is said (Ecclesiastes 12:7) ״וְהָרוּחַ תָּשׁוּב אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר נְתָנָהּ״, and they of blessed memory expounded (Shabbat 152a) ״Give it to Him as He gave it to you״ — can the depositors exempt themselves with the claim of ״its owner is with it״? For they of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 93a): even if he said to him ״Give me water to drink״ and he requested of him his need, behold this is שְׁאֵלָה בִּבְעָלִים, and the same holds for all guardians. And indeed the Master preceded to sustain and provide for [man] from the day man came into being, and it is He who breathes into man's nostrils the breath of life and attends to man's needs — apart from what He does for the maintenance of the world, renewing every day the work of creation. Therefore it is called ״its owner is with it״ from beginning to end, and so the guardians of souls are exempt even though they do not return the soul as it was given." "<b>And I say</b> to the soul of man: do not rely on the oppression of this law, for two reasons it will not exempt with this claim. The first: for here it is explicitly explained that the guardians are liable, and every condition of guardianship is valid, as they said regarding one who hires workers (Bava Metzi'a 94a): the owner of the deposit may stipulate with an unpaid guardian that he be liable like a borrower. And the reason is that every condition concerning money is valid, and likewise the depositor may stipulate with the guardian that he be liable even when the owner is with him, and this does not constitute stipulating against what is written in the Torah in such a manner — as is evident from that which Shmuel said in tractate Makkot (Makkot 3a) regarding ״on condition that you not release it in the seventh year״ — the seventh year does not release it, and they of blessed memory said that the reason is that every condition concerning money is valid. And in the situation before us, it is as though He stipulated explicitly concerning the matter, from the fact that He fixed punishments for every transgressor of what was deposited, and further He added to adjure concerning the matter." "Second: you should know that the judges of the earth do not judge all liabilities but a very small fraction of the many, and all the cases the Judge of all the earth will judge in justice. And who will say that one who is negligent with his fellow's property, while the owner is with him, will not be judged by the Judge — just as He judges many, many cases where people are exempt under human law but liable under the laws of Heaven, etc., and as I explained above on the verse (Exodus 21:12) ״מוֹת יוּמָת״ — that HaShem does not command the judges of the earth to execute all those liable to death, etc. And you will find something analogous to this, that HaShem commanded: an unpaid guardian, a paid guardian, and a borrower are exempt in the case of consecrated property, even if they were negligent. And even according to the Rambam, who distinguished to hold one liable for negligence with land and slaves, it appears from his words that he would concede regarding consecrated property that one is exempt even for negligence, for the principle of diminution applies to all guardians. And from this you learn that it is not permitted and permissible — on the contrary, his punishment is great, for he showed contempt for the property of Heaven — only that HaShem did not fix its law for earthly judges and removed its judgment to before Him. And there are still further matters of Elohim according to the inner truth of the Torah, and their place is not here."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:44.349392957Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}